    <h2 id="howworks">
      How Co-Invest Works
    </h2>

    <p>
      The service is designed around a conversational workflow. Instead of
      filling out a traditional order ticket first, a user can begin with a
      natural-language research question. The AI can then retrieve supported
      market information, discuss the available context, and formulate a
      potential trade idea.
    </p>

    <p>
      When the workflow reaches an actual trading action, Liquid's current
      Co-Invest documentation states that the proposed order is presented for
      explicit user confirmation. The confirmation step is designed to show
      important order details before the transaction is submitted.
    </p>

    <p>
      This creates an important separation between analysis and execution:
      the assistant may help formulate a trade, but the user remains
      responsible for deciding whether the proposed transaction should
      actually be submitted.
    </p>


    <h3>
      Typical workflow
    </h3>

    <ol>

      <li>
        Connect the supported AI client to Co-Invest.
      </li>

      <li>
        Authenticate the Liquid account and approve the requested permissions.
      </li>

      <li>
        Ask the assistant to research a market or portfolio question.
      </li>

      <li>
        Review the resulting analysis and supporting information.
      </li>

      <li>
        If appropriate, request a trade proposal.
      </li>

      <li>
        Review the proposed order details.
      </li>

      <li>
        Explicitly confirm or reject the order.
      </li>

    </ol>


    <h2 id="features">
      Key Features
    </h2>

    <ul>

      <li>
        <strong>Conversational market research:</strong>
        Users can ask questions about markets using natural language.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Market information:</strong>
        Liquid's documentation describes access to information such as
        price, funding, open interest, positioning and liquidation-related
        data for supported markets.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Portfolio context:</strong>
        The service can expose account and position information to the
        connected assistant.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Trade proposals:</strong>
        The assistant can formulate potential orders based on the user's
        request and available market/account information.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Explicit confirmation:</strong>
        Standard Co-Invest trading actions require the user to confirm the
        proposed order before submission.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Charts and market views:</strong>
        Liquid documents price charts, order-book views and market
        overviews as part of the supported workflow.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Paper trading:</strong>
        Users can test the workflow using simulated funds according to
        Liquid's documentation.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Multi-asset coverage:</strong>
        Liquid describes support across crypto and additional financial
        markets available through its platform.
      </li>

    </ul>


    <h2 id="research">
      AI Market Research
    </h2>

    <p>
      One of Co-Invest's more interesting characteristics is the attempt to
      combine AI interpretation with market-specific information. A generic
      language model may be able to explain what funding rates or open
      interest mean, but an integrated trading workflow can potentially use
      current platform data as part of the conversation.
    </p>

    <p>
      Liquid's documentation describes market research capabilities that
      include live pricing information, funding rates, open interest,
      positioning information, liquidation clusters and recent news.
    </p>

    <p>
      For an analyst, this can reduce the amount of manual context switching
      involved in a preliminary market review. For example, instead of
      opening several pages and then asking an AI to interpret manually
      collected information, the user can request a structured market
      assessment within the supported workflow.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">

      <strong>Important:</strong>
      More data does not automatically mean a better investment decision.
      Market information can be incomplete, delayed, incorrectly interpreted,
      or outweighed by events that occur after the analysis. AI-generated
      conclusions should therefore be treated as research output rather than
      guaranteed trading signals.

    </div>


    <h2 id="trading">
      Trading &amp; Order Confirmation
    </h2>

    <p>
      Co-Invest goes beyond research by connecting the AI conversation to
      trading functionality on Liquid. The documentation describes support
      for market and limit orders, positions, order management and optional
      risk-management parameters such as take-profit and stop-loss settings,
      subject to the capabilities and rules of the platform.
    </p>

    <p>
      A particularly important design feature is the confirmation step.
      According to Liquid's documentation, a proposed trade is displayed
      before it is submitted, allowing the user to inspect details such as
      direction, size and leverage before confirming.
    </p>

    <p>
      From a safety perspective, this is preferable to a workflow where an
      ordinary conversational message automatically becomes a live order
      without a visible approval step.
    </p>


    <div class="risk-box">

      <strong>Risk warning:</strong>
      A confirmation screen is not the same thing as protection from losses.
      The user can still confirm an unsuitable trade. Leverage can amplify
      both gains and losses, and perpetual contracts can carry additional
      funding and liquidation risks.

    </div>


