    <h2 id="paper">
      Paper Trading
    </h2>

    <p>
      Paper trading is one of the most useful features for users who want to
      understand an AI-assisted trading workflow without immediately putting
      real capital at risk.
    </p>

    <p>
      Liquid's documentation describes a simulated mode using a virtual
      balance while continuing to reference live market conditions. This can
      allow users to experiment with prompts, position sizing and order
      workflows before switching to live trading.
    </p>

    <h3>
      Why paper trading matters
    </h3>

    <ul>

      <li>
        It lets users understand how the AI proposes trades.
      </li>

      <li>
        It provides an opportunity to test prompt quality.
      </li>

      <li>
        It can reveal misunderstandings about leverage or order size.
      </li>

      <li>
        It helps users become familiar with confirmation screens.
      </li>

      <li>
        It provides a lower-risk environment for learning the interface.
      </li>

    </ul>

    <div class="callout">

      <strong>Editorial view:</strong>
      Paper trading should be the preferred starting point for anyone new
      to AI-assisted execution. A successful simulated trade does not prove
      that a strategy will remain profitable with real money.

    </div>


    <h2 id="security">
      Permissions &amp; Account Controls
    </h2>

    <p>
      Connecting a trading account to an AI system naturally raises security
      and permission questions. According to Liquid's documentation, the
      standard Co-Invest connection uses authentication and separates
      read-related capabilities from trading permissions.
    </p>

    <p>
      Liquid also states that Co-Invest cannot withdraw or transfer funds,
      change account settings, or submit a standard trade without the
      required confirmation step.
    </p>

    <p>
      These controls reduce some categories of operational risk, but they do
      not eliminate the need for account security. Users should review the
      permissions displayed during connection and avoid approving a
      connection if the requested access differs from what they expect.
    </p>


    <h3>
      Security checklist
    </h3>

    <ul>

      <li>
        Confirm that you are connecting through the legitimate Liquid domain.
      </li>

      <li>
        Read the authorization screen before approving access.
      </li>

      <li>
        Never share passwords or authentication codes with an AI assistant.
      </li>

      <li>
        Start with paper trading when learning the workflow.
      </li>

      <li>
        Use conservative risk limits when testing live trading.
      </li>

      <li>
        Periodically review connected applications and permissions.
      </li>

    </ul>


