    <h2 id="usecases">
      Investment Research Use Cases
    </h2>


    <h3>
      1. Initial market screening
    </h3>

    <p>
      A trader can use the conversational interface to request a broad
      market overview and then narrow the research to a smaller group of
      assets. This can be useful during the first stage of research when
      the goal is to identify what deserves further investigation.
    </p>


    <h3>
      2. Combining several market signals
    </h3>

    <p>
      Instead of looking at price alone, a research request can ask the
      assistant to consider multiple available signals such as funding,
      open interest, positioning, news and market structure.
    </p>


    <h3>
      3. Portfolio-aware research
    </h3>

    <p>
      When account information is available to the connected workflow,
      research can potentially be framed around the user's existing
      positions rather than analyzing every market in isolation.
    </p>


    <h3>
      4. Trade-plan drafting
    </h3>

    <p>
      AI can help turn a research hypothesis into a structured plan covering
      direction, position size, invalidation conditions and potential
      exit levels. The final decision should remain with the user.
    </p>


    <h3>
      5. Learning trading concepts
    </h3>

    <p>
      Conversational interfaces can also be useful for education. A user
      can ask the assistant to explain leverage, funding, liquidation,
      margin or order types in plain language before attempting a trade.
    </p>


    <h2 id="limitations">
      Limitations &amp; Risks
    </h2>

    <p>
      Co-Invest's integration with market data and trading functionality is
      useful, but it does not remove the fundamental risks associated with
      financial markets.
    </p>

    <ul>

      <li>
        <strong>AI can be wrong:</strong>
        A fluent explanation is not proof that the underlying conclusion is
        correct.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Markets can change rapidly:</strong>
        A market setup can become invalid shortly after an analysis is made.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Leverage increases risk:</strong>
        Leveraged positions can amplify losses as well as gains.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Data has limitations:</strong>
        No market-data system guarantees complete or perfect information.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Execution does not equal profitability:</strong>
        Faster order placement does not make an investment thesis correct.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Human judgment remains important:</strong>
        Users are still responsible for the trades they approve.
      </li>

    </ul>


    <div class="risk-box">

      <strong>Who should be especially careful?</strong>

      <p style="margin-top:10px;">
        Beginners, users unfamiliar with leverage, and anyone trading money
        they cannot afford to lose should be particularly cautious. Paper
        trading and education are more appropriate starting points than
        immediately using live leveraged positions.

      </p>

    </div>


